    answer: "The answer: On commercial, public, and multi-family projects in Rhode Island, the structural drawings, foundation plans, and life-safety engineering sheets must be stamped by a professional engineer licensed by the Rhode Island Board of Registration for Professional Engineers. Rhode Island enforces the Rhode Island State Building Code (SBC-1, based on the IBC), and the code's registered design professional provisions decide which sheets carry the seal.\\n\\nThe sealed sets follow the building's systems. The structural set — foundations, framing, the lateral system, and connection details — is stamped on virtually every commercial project. Civil and site drawings carry the seal when grading, drainage, or coastal work is engineered. MEP drawings are sealed by the licensed designers of each system on larger buildings. Rhode Island's septic rules add their own drawing set: the Department of Environmental Management requires engineered OWTS (onsite wastewater treatment system) designs by a licensed designer or PE for systems beyond conventional residential. Pre-engineered trusses arrive with manufacturer-sealed shop drawings referenced into the permit set.\\n\\nRhode Island-specific triggers are coastal. The Coastal Resources Management Council (CRMC) holds jurisdiction over tidal waters and the adjacent shoreline — projects in CRMC's reach need a CRMC assent with engineered drawings showing setbacks, buffers, and coastal construction details, a separate approval track from the building permit. Hurricane wind design shapes the structural and envelope drawings: coastal Rhode Island designs for high wind speeds with opening protection and continuous load-path detailing the reviewer checks. Providence's and Newport's historic district commissions add design review drawing packages for downtown and waterfront work, and Providence's permitting office publishes the sealed-sheet expectations for the capital's projects.\\n\\nGray areas sit inland. Straightforward one- and two-family homes outside the coastal zone and floodplain often permit without an engineer stamp, and small residential renovations may need only architectural drawings. But CRMC jurisdiction, FEMA flood zones, and historic districts can each pull an otherwise simple project into engineered drawings — the town building official and a CRMC jurisdiction check settle it before design starts.",
    directAnswer: "In Rhode Island, commercial, public, and multi-family projects require a Rhode Island-licensed PE stamp on structural, foundation, and life-safety drawing sets. Coastal projects add CRMC review with engineered drawings, and hurricane wind speeds shape the structural and envelope design statewide.",
