        heading: "Rhode Island-Specific Stamp Triggers",
        body: "CRMC jurisdiction is Rhode Island's defining trigger. The Coastal Resources Management Council reviews work in tidal waters, on the shoreline, and within its buffer zones — and the assent application is an engineered drawing package: site plans with CRMC setback lines, coastal construction details, erosion control, and stormwater management. The CRMC track runs parallel to the municipal building permit, and the building official will not finalize a coastal permit without the assent. Hurricane wind design is the structural companion: the wind speed maps push coastal structural design hard, and the drawings must show the continuous load path — roof-to-wall-to-foundation connections, opening protection schedules, and the uplift detailing the reviewer verifies.\\n\\nHistoric and urban review add the remaining triggers. Providence's Historic District Commission and Newport's Historic District Commission require design review drawing packages — elevations, materials, and compatibility documentation — for work in the districts. Providence's permitting office, handling the state's largest project volume, publishes submittal checklists naming the sealed sheets each review expects. FEMA flood zones along Narragansett Bay, the Sakonnet River, and Block Island bring elevation certificates and flood-resistant construction details into the sealed set.",
